As used in this Ordinance, the following terms shall be construed to mean:

(a) Agricultural Chemical - refers to any fertilizer, pesticide, fungicide, herbicide, insecticide, or other chemical substance utilized in agricultural production and crop protection;

(b) Aerial Spraying - refers to the application, spraying, or dispersion of agricultural chemicals from an airplane, helicopter, drone, or any airborne aircraft;

(c) Commercial Plantation - refers to any agricultural land or farm holding dedicated to the commercial cultivation of export crops such as bananas, pineapples, or oil palm;

(d) Buffer Zone - refers to a physical setback area of land established between a commercial plantation boundary and adjoining residential areas, public roads, or bodies of water;

(e) Ground Spraying - refers to the manual or mechanized application of agricultural chemicals using tractor-mounted boom sprayers, power sprayers, or knapsack sprayers operated from the ground.

All ground-based chemical spraying operations within commercial plantations shall be subject to the following regulations:

(a) Spraying operations shall not be conducted during periods of heavy wind or adverse weather conditions that cause excessive drift toward neighboring communities;

(b) Plantation operators shall provide all farm sprayers and agricultural workers with appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE), including protective clothing, gloves, and respirators;

(c) Spraying personnel must undergo proper safety training in chemical handling and application approved by the City Agriculturist's Office;

(d) Chemical storage and mixing areas must be situated away from residential settlements and drainage channels.

Any person, plantation operator, manager, or corporate officer who violates the provisions of this Ordinance shall, upon conviction, be penalized as follows:

(a) First Offense: A fine of One Thousand Pesos (Php 1,000.00);

(b) Second Offense: A fine of Three Thousand Pesos (Php 3,000.00);

(c) Third and Subsequent Offenses: A fine of Five Thousand Pesos (Php 5,000.00) or imprisonment for a period of six (6) months, or both such fine and imprisonment at the discretion of the court.

In addition to the foregoing penalties, the business permit of the commercial plantation may be suspended or revoked upon recommendation of the City Legal Officer.

For purposes of this Ordinance, the following terms shall be defined as follows:

(a) Overland Transport Terminal - refers to the Davao City Overland Transport Terminal (DCOTT) and all public and private bus depots, van terminals, and public utility staging areas within Davao City;

(b) Terminal Operator - refers to any government office, private corporation, or person operating or managing a public transport terminal;

(c) Security Personnel - refers to organic security guards, members of the Public Safety and Security Office (PSSO), and police officers assigned to maintain order within terminal compounds;

(d) Passenger Baggage - refers to luggage, backpacks, packages, and parcels brought into terminal premises by traveling passengers;

(e) Public Utility Vehicle (PUV) - refers to buses, passenger vans, and jeepneys duly authorized by the LTFRB to transport passengers for compensation.

All terminal operators shall install, operate, and maintain electronic security screening equipment at all primary passenger entrance gates:

(a) Walk-through metal detectors and hand-held metal detector wands for screening passengers entering passenger waiting areas;

(b) Baggage inspection tables or conveyorized X-ray machines for non-intrusive examination of passenger luggage, backpacks, and large parcels;

(c) Closed-Circuit Television (CCTV) cameras installed at strategic locations including entry gates, ticket counters, and boarding bays, conforming to the provisions of the Davao City CCTV Ordinance;

(d) Adequate physical signage informing passengers of security regulations and prohibited items posted in prominent areas.

All terminal operators shall provide clean, dignified, and functional amenities for traveling passengers without charging comfort room user fees:

(a) Separate, clean toilet facilities for male and female passengers with continuous running water and hand soap;

(b) Adequate and well-lit waiting areas with comfortable seating and natural or mechanical ventilation;

(c) Clear priority assistance lanes and accessible facilities for senior citizens, persons with disabilities (PWDs), and pregnant women pursuant to Batas Pambansa Blg. 344;

(d) Clean drinking water stations accessible to commuters within waiting lounges.

Any terminal operator, manager, or person who violates the provisions of this Ordinance shall, upon conviction, be punished as follows:

(a) First Offense: A fine of One Thousand Pesos (Php 1,000.00);

(b) Second Offense: A fine of Three Thousand Pesos (Php 3,000.00);

(c) Third and Subsequent Offenses: A fine of Five Thousand Pesos (Php 5,000.00) or imprisonment for a period of up to six (6) months, or both such fine and imprisonment at the discretion of the court.

For repeated non-compliance by private terminal operators, the City Mayor may revoke the business permit of the terminal facility upon recommendation of the City Legal Officer.

As used in this Ordinance, the following terms shall mean:

(a) Stationary Source - refers to any industrial plant, factory, boiler, generator, furnace, or commercial facility that emits smoke, gas, or particulate matter into the atmosphere;

(b) Air Pollution - refers to any alteration of the physical, chemical, or biological properties of the atmosphere that endangers health or creates a public nuisance;

(c) Pollution Control Facility - refers to any baghouse filter, electrostatic precipitator, wet scrubber, or cyclone separator designed to reduce industrial air emissions;

(d) Dark Smoke - refers to smoke emissions that equal or exceed Ringelmann Chart density standards for excessive atmospheric obscurity;

(e) Industrial Establishment - refers to factories, mills, batching plants, and processing plants situated within the industrial and commercial zones of Davao City.

As used in this Ordinance, the following terms shall mean:

(a) Electric Bicycle (E-Bike) - refers to any two- or three-wheeled vehicle equipped with an electric motor and functional pedals;

(b) Electric Scooter (E-Scooter) - refers to a stand-up or sit-down light two-wheeled vehicle powered by an electric motor;

(c) City Streets - refers to roads and streets within Davao City not designated as national or provincial highways;

(d) Dedicated Bicycle Lanes - refers to designated lanes on public roads reserved for bicycles and light mobility conveyances;

(e) CTTMO - refers to the City Transport and Traffic Management Office of Davao City.

All riders and operators of electric bicycles and e-scooters traversing public roads in Davao City shall observe the following safety requirements:

(a) Mandatory wearing of protective bicycle or motorcycle safety helmets at all times;

(b) Equipping vehicles with operational white front headlights and red rear reflectors or tail lights during nighttime operation from 6:00 PM to 6:00 AM;

(c) Installation of functional mechanical brakes and an audible bell or horn;

(d) Prohibition against carrying passengers beyond the designed manufacturer capacity of the vehicle.

Any person issued a citation ticket for violation of Section 5 or Section 7 who does not wish to contest the citation may avail of the No-Contest provision by paying an administrative fine to the City Treasurer within seventy-two (72) hours:

(a) First Offense: Three Hundred Pesos (Php 300.00);

(b) Second Offense: Five Hundred Pesos (Php 500.00);

(c) Third Offense: One Thousand Pesos (Php 1,000.00).

As used in this Ordinance, the following terms shall mean:

(a) Basic Necessities - refers to essential food items including rice, corn, fresh milk, fresh eggs, pork, beef, poultry meat, fresh vegetables, root crops, and cooking oil;

(b) Price Gouging - refers to the act of selling basic food necessities at prices significantly higher than the prevailing market price without justifiable economic reason;

(c) Hoarding - refers to the undue accumulation and holding back of basic food items from commercial channels of trade to create an artificial shortage and hike prices;

(d) Public Market - refers to city-owned public markets including Bankerohan, Agdao, Calinan, and Toril, as well as privately operated wet markets in Davao City;

(e) LPCC - refers to the Local Price Coordinating Council of Davao City.

For purposes of this Ordinance, the following terms shall mean:

(a) Closed-Circuit Television (CCTV) System - refers to a video surveillance system in which signals are transmitted to monitors and recording devices within a closed network;

(b) Commercial Establishment - refers to banks, shopping malls, supermarkets, hotels, restaurants, bars, pawnshops, and business establishments operating in Davao City;

(c) Public and Common Areas - refers to entry and exit doors, lobbies, parking areas, and transaction counters accessible to the general public;

(d) PSSO - refers to the Public Safety and Security Office of the City Government of Davao.

Any establishment or person violating this Ordinance shall, upon conviction, be penalized as follows:

(a) First Offense: A fine of One Thousand Pesos (Php 1,000.00);

(b) Second Offense: A fine of Three Thousand Pesos (Php 3,000.00);

(c) Third and Subsequent Offenses: A fine of Five Thousand Pesos (Php 5,000.00) and revocation of Mayor's Business Permit until full compliance is certified.

As used in this Ordinance, the following terms shall mean:

(a) Coastal Waters - refers to the municipal waters, foreshore areas, and marine waters within the territorial jurisdiction of Davao City;

(b) Commercial Vessel - refers to motorized cargo ships, passenger vessels, tugboats, and commercial fishing vessels docking in city ports;

(c) Bilge Water - refers to water that collects in the lowest compartment of a ship's hull, frequently containing oil and chemical residues;

(d) Port Wharves - refers to Santa Ana Wharf, Sasa Port, and private docking wharves operating in Davao City;

(e) City ENRO - refers to the City Environment and Natural Resources Office.

As used in this Ordinance, the following terms shall mean:

(a) Commercial High-Rise Building - refers to any commercial, residential, or mixed-use building in Davao City having five (5) storeys or more in height;

(b) Structural Audit - refers to a comprehensive structural safety assessment and engineering inspection of a building's load-bearing columns, beams, foundations, and shear walls;

(c) Building Owner - refers to the registered owner, condominium corporation, or authorized property management administrator of a commercial high-rise building;

(d) OCBO - refers to the Office of the City Building Official of Davao City.

As used in this Ordinance, the following terms shall mean:

(a) Quarry Resources - refers to any common stone, gravel, sand, earth, and volcanic rock extracted for construction purposes;

(b) Commercial Sand and Gravel Permit - refers to an extraction permit issued by the City Mayor for quarry areas not exceeding five (5) hectares pursuant to Section 138 of RA 7160;

(c) Quarry Operator - refers to any individual, cooperative, or enterprise granted a valid quarry extraction permit by the City Government;

(d) CQRB - refers to the City Quarry Regulatory Board of Davao City;

(e) Riverbed - refers to the natural bed and banks of rivers within the city, including the Davao River, Talomo River, and Lasang River.

Section 45 of the 2005 Revenue Code of Davao City is hereby amended to adjust the annual regulatory permit fees on commercial establishments to reflect the reasonable administrative cost of regulation and inspection:

(a) Wholesale and retail merchants with capital of more than P500,000: One Thousand Pesos (Php 1,000.00);

(b) Manufacturing and industrial processing plants: Two Thousand Five Hundred Pesos (Php 2,500.00);

(c) Commercial banks and financial intermediaries: Three Thousand Pesos (Php 3,000.00).
